abstract:
For a given stability condition <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\sigma $</tex> on a triangulated category, we define a <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\sigma $</tex>-exceptional collection as an Ext-exceptional collection, whose elements are <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\sigma $</tex>-semistable with phases contained in an open interval of length 1. If a full <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\sigma $</tex>-exceptional collection exists, then <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\sigma $</tex> is generated by this collection in a procedure described by E. Macrì. Constructing <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\sigma $</tex>-exceptional collections in hereditary <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\hom $</tex>-finite categories, we introduce certain conditions on the Ext-nontrivial couples (exceptional objects with <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">${\mathrm Ext}^1(X,Y)
eq 0$</tex>, <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">${\mathrm Ext}^1(Y,X)
eq 0$</tex>). We study exceptional representations of the quivers <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$Q_1 = \circ\overset{
earrow^{\circ}
warrow}{\longrightarrow}\circ,\ Q_2= egin{matrix} \circ ightarrow \circ \ \uparrow \ \uparrow \ \circ ightarrow \circ \end{matrix}$</tex> and show that the needed conditions do hold in <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">${\mathrm Rep}_k(Q_1)$</tex>, <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">${\mathrm Rep}_k(Q_2)$</tex>. Finally, any <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\sigma \in {\mathrm Stab}(D^b(Q_1))$</tex> is shown to admit a full <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">$\sigma $</tex>-exceptional collection. This implies that the space <tex xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">${\mathrm Stab}(D^b(Q_1))$</tex> is connected.
